  <h1>Lemon Law Guides</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>State lemon laws and the federal Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act give vehicle consumers powerful rights — including mandatory attorney fee shifting that makes legal representation effectively free in successful cases. These guides explain how lemon law works, what the qualification thresholds are, and what consumers should do once they believe they have a lemon.</p>
  <p>All guides are educational materials only, not legal advice. Because lemon laws vary significantly by state, consult a licensed lemon law attorney in your state for advice specific to your situation. Most offer free case evaluations.</p>

  <h2>How lemon law works</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/how-lemon-law-works">How Lemon Law Works</a></h3>
  <p>Qualification thresholds (repair attempts and days out of service), the repurchase formula including mileage offset, the attorney fee shifting mechanism that makes legal representation free to prevailing consumers, and the manufacturer arbitration process.</p>

  <h2>State-by-state differences</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/lemon-law-by-state">Lemon Law by State</a></h3>
  <p>How California&rsquo;s Song-Beverly Act, New York&rsquo;s lemon law, Texas&rsquo;s procedures, Florida&rsquo;s coverage period, and other major states handle lemon law claims — and how they differ from the federal Magnuson-Moss standard.</p>

  <h2>What to do with a lemon</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/what-to-do-with-a-lemon">What to Do With a Lemon</a></h3>
  <p>Step-by-step: documenting repair visits, sending written notice to the manufacturer, hiring a lemon law attorney, and what to expect from the claim process.</p>

  <h2>Common misconceptions</h2>
  <h3><a href="/guides/lemon-law-misconceptions">Common Lemon Law Misconceptions</a></h3>
  <p>Five myths that cause consumers to underestimate their rights or mishandle their claims: mileage offset misunderstandings, used vehicle exclusions, mandatory arbitration confusion, attorney fee concerns, and waiting too long to assert rights.</p>

